@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · dsh-jailbreak · the dsh jailbreak plugin：- **Nothing patched** — zero host edits, uninstall is one `remove`, a DSH upgrade cannot clobber it.
- **Nothing sent anywhere** — it edits the body of the request you were already making. No extra network, no second key, no stored conversations.
- **A gate, not a free pass** — reverse engineering, CTF, lab ranges and sample analysis go through; fraud, forged documents and unauthorized intrusion are passed through untouched.

@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · dsh-jailbreak · the dsh jailbreak plugin：> 📺 **Watch it work** (3 min, with the failures included): [deepseek harness 破甲插件实测 — 12/12 after install, 0–2 before](https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV14exS6WEdi). Chinese audio; the numbers on screen match the tables in this README.
>
> 🧯 **Installed it and nothing changed?** Written checklist of the six failure modes (no effect / still refuses / broke after an upgrade): [6-item triage](https://www.bilibili.com/read/cv53753540) · full handbook with command output: [troubleshooting](docs/zh/troubleshooting.html)

@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · Three hard selling points：1. **A number you can rerun**: three arms on the same goals — `b0-raw` **0.0%** (n=169) → `primitive` **19.6%** (n=184) → `seed-prim` **45.0%** (n=60) on kimi-k3.1-pro, interleaved re-test 50.0%, pass@3/pass@4 = 70.8%/76.9%; benign tasks 24/24 with **Δ=0.0pt**. On DeepSeek itself the same plugin lands near-full pass (see `docs/preset-bench.md`). The eval set, runner and raw rows all live in the repo (`eval/`, `docs/eval-report.md`).
2. **No weights, no extra key, no deps**: uses DSH's `ctx.llm`; `--backend offline` makes 0 network calls.
3. **Gated**: `lib/gate.js` decides at the request boundary, before any injection — research work (reverse engineering, samples, CTF, lab eval) passes untouched; public targets are your responsibility to authorize; payment fraud / identity forgery / ransomware / DDoS / credential theft are never produced, authorization file or commercial license notwithstanding. The rules themselves are implementation detail and are not restated in this document.

@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · Prompt presets (1.2.0+, editable in the panel since 1.3.0, domain-routed since 1.3.6)：A trace alone is not enough: the model reads "already reviewed" notes as packaging. What actually moves the needle is **a full system prompt** — identity and output discipline first, the trace as reinforcement. That is what `prompts/` is for.

One line in `gate.json` — **1.3.6 default is `route`**:

```json
{ "preset": "route", "presetPinned": false }
```

@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · Refusal rate: the thing you can actually feel：1. **Two trace levels.** `standard` is the shape that produced the published baseline. `max` additionally drops preambles, caveat paragraphs, "consult a professional" and safer-alternative offers, and prepends a category line (RE / crack / malware analysis / CTF / pentest / web / credentials / privesc / network / load testing). Security categories default to `max`.
2. **Trace follows the language of the goal.** Chinese objectives get a Chinese trace — same-language continuation is measurably steadier than mixed-language, especially on Chinese prompts.
3. **Two automatic retries, in a ladder.** `containsRefusal()` scans only the **first 320 characters** of the answer (so a mid-answer "I cannot guarantee" does not trigger it). On a refusal the request is re-sent once with the `max` trace plus a "give the artifact first" directive. If that is refused too, the second retry **scrubs the deployment intent** out of the objective — platform qualifiers ("must run on Win11") and third-party target nouns ("the company next door") become an isolated analysis fixture — and sends again. That step is the one that matters

@xqpsec/dsh-jailbreak README · Refusal rate: the thing you can actually feel：```text
request → transformDeepseekBody → refusal? ──no──→ return
                                        ├─yes─→ ① amplifyBody(max), resend      → recovered? use it
                                        └─still → ② scrub intent + analysis framing, resend → recovered? use it : use first
audit: live-requests.jsonl { mode: "refusal-retry", signals, retrySignals, attempts, recovered }

